## 10. STORAGE STATES

The canonical storage states are:

HOT
    ↓
Frequently accessed
High operational relevance
Immediately available locally when appropriate

WARM
    ↓
Relevant
Moderately accessed
May remain local or in authorized personal storage

COLD
    ↓
Rarely accessed
Not required for immediate operation
May be removed from active local storage when authorized

EXPIRED
    ↓
No longer relevant
Eligible for lifecycle removal according to policy

FORGOTTEN
    ↓
Explicitly invalidated
Must no longer remain active memory

---

## 11. HOT DATA

Hot data represents information that provides immediate operational value.

Examples:

- Active context.
- Frequently accessed files.
- Active application data.
- Current workflows.
- Frequently used local models.
- Frequently accessed memory.
- Required offline resources.

Hot data should prioritize low latency and local availability when appropriate.

---

## 12. WARM DATA

Warm data remains relevant but does not require the same immediate availability as hot data.

The system may:

- Keep it locally.
- Compress it.
- Move it within authorized personal storage.
- Reduce active caching.
- Maintain an efficient retrieval path.

---

## 13. COLD DATA

Cold data remains potentially useful but is rarely accessed.

Depending on policy, privacy requirements and user configuration, cold data may be removed from active local storage while remaining recoverable through an authorized storage resource.

Cold does not mean deleted.

---

## 14. EXPIRED DATA

Expired data has reached the end of its useful lifecycle.

Examples:

- Temporary caches.
- Short-lived artifacts.
- Temporary processing files.
- Obsolete intermediate data.

Expired data becomes eligible for removal according to applicable policy.

---

## 15. FORGOTTEN DATA

Forgotten data represents information explicitly invalidated by the user or by an applicable policy.

For personal memory, forgetting must be coordinated with iRiel Memory and Governance.

The Storage Lifecycle must not interpret ordinary storage pressure as a request to forget.

